    "answer": {
      "short": "$f(x)=x(x-1)(x+1)=x^3-x$ is odd, but $f(-1)=f(0)=f(1)=0$. It fails the horizontal line test, so its inverse is not a function.",
      "worked": "A counterexample is $f(x)=x(x-1)(x+1)=x^3-x$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=(-x)^3-(-x)\\\\f(-x)&=-x^3+x\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x)\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is odd. However, $f(-1)=f(0)=f(1)=0$, so the horizontal line $y=0$ meets its graph three times. Its inverse is not a function.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
